                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "model-2800":</h3><p><b>Model 2800</b> is one of two <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/rectangular-diaphragm">rectangular condenser capsule</a> designs from Sweden&#8217;s <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Pearl/DS-60">Pearl Microphone Lab</a>. The housing measures 35mm x 17mm, with a suspended diaphragm area of 29mm x 14mm. </p><p>The diaphragm surface area, 406 square mm, is 18% smaller than the surface area of a typical round &#8220;large-diaphragm&#8221; capsule (diameter 25mm). Nonetheless, it is considered a &#8220;large diaphragm&#8221; capsule because in its longest dimension it measures 25mm or more.</p><p>PML uses 6-micron Mylar diaphragms with a thin coating of vaporized aluminum. The diaphragms are covered with a perforated metal plate. Pearl technicians claim the metal plate does not affect the capsule&#8217;s frequency response, but rather provides mechanical stability.</p><p>The history of the 2800 capsule is contested. According to Pearl, the 2800 is identical to the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/model-2700">Model 2700</a> capsule originally designed by Rune Rosander. According to <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Milab">Milab</a>, which owns the rights to Rosander&#8217;s 2700 capsule, the 2800 was redesigned by PML staff after Rosander&#8217;s product designs were transferred to Milab. In either case, the two capsules are similar in size, aspect ratio, and construction: dual-backplate, Mylar diaphragms, aluminum coating, perforated metal cover, etc.</p>
